WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.020
Hello, this is Manner Code.

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:05.350
Software is complicated.

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:08.890
First of all, there is a lot of code to write. The

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.710
example project created for this series alone

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:15.270
has over 12,000 lines of code. If it is an

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:18.320
actual operating service, even a

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:21.530
small service, it will easily exceed 100,000 lines.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:25.300
100,000 lines is the amount of code in 20 novels.

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:29.560
Writing a long code is not the end. You have to

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.540
keep fixing the code you wrote. This means that if you

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.800
fix A, B breaks down, and if you

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:39.130
fix B, A and C break down. To use an analogy, it is like a horse

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:44.260
crying while trapped in a Rasputica.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.360


00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:50.550
At this point, you feel the need for design.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.010
However, when you actually try to write a design document, you realize that

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.490
expressing the thoughts in your head in detail is

00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:01.260
not as easy as you thought. Even a sheet music, a

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.600
document that visually records sound or music, is

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:06.700
this complicated. It is not easy to express not only music but also various concepts such as

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:10.610
finance and logistics

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:14.060
and abstract user requirements

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:17.220
in a concrete model.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:19.490
Fortunately, UML was born in the late 1990s

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:23.200
to overcome the difficulty of modeling abstract concepts.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:26.860


00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:30.120
Grady Bouch led the way.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:34.390
We use UML with anticipation. Let's

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.990
draw a class diagram.  I

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:38.760
also draw sequence diagrams,

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:39.980
but when I

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:43.160
actually try to design them, it does

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.130
n't seem to be much different from coding them directly.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:46.520
Why is that?

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:50.910
This is because the thought process is accustomed to implementation. It is

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:54.170


00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:57.590
because it transfers implementation-level thoughts into UML rather than gradually concretizing abstract concepts. Just as

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:00.270
knowing how to read sheet music does

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:01.950
not enable you to compose music,

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:03.850
learning UML does

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:06.170
not give you design skills.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:09.300
Therefore, in this series, I will show you

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.580


00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.420
how to concretize abstract requirements and

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:16.290
connect code through a real project. I

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.010


00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:20.930
chose movie ticket reservations as the domain.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:25.200
Movie ticket reservations are a familiar domain for most people, so they are easy to understand and

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.710
include various functions such as login, payment, and seat selection, making them a

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:31.580
suitable example. The

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.890
basic topic of this article is software design, but I will

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:40.350
also cover test-driven development.

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:44.890
Modern development environments basically support testing tools. Even if you

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.320
look at famous open source projects, they

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:51.130
have a certain level of testing.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:56.200
This means that testing is important and highly utilized.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:58.120
However, in reality,

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.590
many cases do not even properly implement test automation, let alone TDD. It is by no means easy to

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:06.260
write meaningful tests without any preparation.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:07.540


00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:12.340
This is possible only when you have software design capabilities.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.270
Therefore, I decided that it would be efficient to cover design and TDD together.

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:18.180


00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:23.450
Now, let's start the first meeting of the project.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:26.470
Since this is a small project, there are

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:28.090
two participants. The

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:32.340
one on the left is the domain expert, and the one on the right is the test automation expert.  I'm a developer. The

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:37.790
domain expert tells us a lot about the system we need to build.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:39.280


00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:42.160
We operate about

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.710
4,000 theaters nationwide, and that number

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:47.220
could increase in the future.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.480
Currently, we only support movie ticket reservations, but we

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.510
plan to add large-scale performance tickets in the future.

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:58.940
And the important thing is that seats cannot be double-booked.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:03.250
In the past, our customer service center had a really hard time due to that problem. We

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:07.670
also have a lot of accumulated movie information and review data, so

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:11.090
this data must be maintained. The

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.730
conversation here is well-organized, but in

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:16.960
reality, more information will be

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:18.600
conveyed in a disorganized manner.

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:22.110
However, we need to listen carefully to the stories that come out at the beginning of the project, because they are the

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:23.780
most important requirements.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.320


00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.810
The developer listens to the domain expert and asks

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:31.290
a few questions. When do you plan to add the

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:34.660
large-scale performance ticket reservation feature that you mentioned

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:36.910
?  I'd like to know if this should be

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:39.910
included in the scope of this project in advance

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:43.590
or if it's not something I should consider yet.

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:46.090
And does keeping existing data

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:48.940
mean not changing the DB system?  Are

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:51.950
there any other services currently sharing the DB?

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:54.980
As you can see from the DB, the

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:57.290
domain expert is not a developer, so the

00:04:57.290 --> 00:04:59.430
technical expressions are vague.

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:02.890
These things need to be carefully checked by the developer. The

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:07.450
domain expert answers the developer's question like this. There is no specific schedule for the

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:10.210
large-scale concert ticket reservation function

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:13.450
yet.

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:15.970
However, I think it would be good if you could consider it a little this time so that we can upgrade without much difficulty when the function is added in the future. The

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:18.700


00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:22.600


00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:27.050
DB is not used anywhere else but our service. As long as the

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:32.840
data is well maintained, changing the system itself is not a big problem. From what I've heard, it

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:35.080


00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:39.400
seems that we don't need to worry about the content regarding large-scale concerts. It is

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:40.790
not the scope of the project, and the

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:43.040
requirements are not clear. I don't know

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:44.930
when it will be needed, but it is an

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:47.710
issue that needs to be reviewed again when it is needed.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:50.910
Even if it is not documented, you should not forget to tell the

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:54.880
domain expert that you will carefully consider large-scale concerts.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:55.960


00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:58.320
Understood.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.630
Then, the large-scale concert ticket reservation function will

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:04.500
not be one of the top requirements for this project.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.220
Instead, we will pay close attention to making it easy to expand the function later during the development process. Do

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:10.190


00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:12.840
n't worry.  It's

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:15.960
important to get along well with someone who believes in you.

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:19.500
Through our conversations so far, we've

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:22.700
summarized the top priorities as follows. We begin the project by interpreting and organizing the

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:25.890
information provided by the domain expert

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:28.410
from a developer's perspective.

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:29.810


00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:32.860


00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:34.980
Once you've roughly established the project's goals with the domain expert, it's

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:37.980
time to name the project.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:41.840
First, ask the domain expert for their opinion.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:45.540
What should we name the project?

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:47.680
It's a movie reservation system.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:50.150
How about just the Movie Booking System?  I do

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:51.430
n't like it. I'll

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:53.570
worry about large-scale performances. Let's

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:56.930
use the Movie Booking System.  yes?

00:06:56.930 --> 00:06:58.180
Okay,

00:06:58.180 --> 00:06:59.950
thank you.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:05.230
Domain experts might want a Korean project name.

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:07.680
However, from a developer's perspective, since

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:09.990
all terms, including the project name, are

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:12.180
directly implemented in the code, it's

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.000
easier to define them in English if possible. It's

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:19.510
important for developers to faithfully follow the domain expert's requests, but they

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:21.430


00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:23.930
also have the responsibility to coordinate the project in an efficient way by considering the entire project. Once the

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:27.760
project name is decided, it's

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:31.380
time to find out who will use the Movie Booking System.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:34.940
Of course, we need to ask the domain expert for this information.

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:38.740
And we know that there are customers and managers.

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:43.280
One element that is easy to miss in the early stages of design

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:44.760
is external dependencies.

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:46.580
External dependencies

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:49.210
could be existing legacy systems or

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:51.420
payment systems.

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:53.670
These dependencies can have a

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:56.760
significant impact on the overall system design, so it's

00:07:56.950 --> 00:08:00.150
important to identify them as early as possible.

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:01.240
Fortunately,

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:04.990
elements such as payment gateways and legacy systems are

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:07.730
already used in existing systems, so they

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:10.360
can be identified relatively easily.

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:14.410
One of the elements that is easy to overlook in dependencies is

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:16.090
external environmental factors.

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:19.060
Korea is in the same time zone, but in a

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:22.430
large country, each theater has a different time zone.

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:25.100
So, the Movie Booking System is

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:28.900
inevitably affected by the environmental factor of time zone.

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:32.760
These factors are easy to miss unless you ask the domain expert directly.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:33.950


00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:37.610
Domain experts are good at identifying these aspects.

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:40.010
Since we might consider it trivial and skip the explanation, now that we have

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:43.640
specified the dependencies, let's

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:47.500
focus on the internals of the Movie Booking System.

00:08:48.430 --> 00:08:52.230
First, let's ask a domain expert what the customer does in the Movie Booking System.

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:54.230


00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:57.880
What does the customer do?

00:08:57.880 --> 00:08:59.970
Customers perform tasks such as searching for movies,

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:01.450
viewing movie details,

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:02.910
selecting a showtime,

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:04.450
selecting a seat,

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:05.920
booking a movie,

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:07.610
checking reservation details, and

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:10.180
canceling a reservation.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:15.750
Are choosing a showtime and selecting a seat part of the process of booking a movie?

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:18.880
And isn't reserving a movie ticket and canceling a reservation

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:22.210
ultimately the same as purchasing a ticket and getting a refund?  That's right.

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:23.620


00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:25.030


00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:27.880
Since this is a familiar domain, you could have asked if

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:31.240
selecting a showtime and selecting a seat were

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:33.200
part of the process of booking a movie ticket.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:34.530


00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:37.220
However, if it's an unfamiliar domain, you'll repeat several refinement processes while

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:40.160
analyzing each use case in detail.

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:43.330


00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:45.350
Depending on the project situation,

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:48.080
this process may be repeated several times, but the more

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:50.580
leisurely and meticulously you analyze, the less trial and

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:54.190
error you'll experience during the design and implementation stages.

00:09:55.850 --> 00:09:57.530
Based on our conversation so far, we've

00:09:57.530 --> 00:10:00.970
organized the customer's use cases into four categories.

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:04.470
Next is the administrator. You

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:08.850
can see that the administrator has five management items.

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:13.620
Personally, I tend to be careful with the word "Manage" in development. It's

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:16.980
too general an expression, so it's

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:19.030
unclear what the job is. This is also the case

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:20.950
here. I'm not sure

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:23.760
how to specifically manage.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:24.810


00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:27.620
Through conversations with domain experts, we

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:31.220
expanded each management item into a specific use case.

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:33.660
In an actual project,

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:35.890
there would be more use cases than this, but I've

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:39.000
simplified it for the sake of explanation. What's

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:42.700
notable here is that creating a showtime also

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:45.900
creates a ticket.

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:49.200
And you

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:53.910
can also see that an external service called a payment gateway is used to purchase or refund tickets.

00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:58.670
I've combined the customer and administrator use cases.

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:01.730
Looking at the entire set of use cases like this,

00:11:01.730 --> 00:11:02.900
something feels missing.

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:05.840
There's adding a theater, but there's nothing

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:08.720
like searching for a theater.

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:12.130
It seems like there are quite a few use cases that are not visible.

00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:15.980
From a domain expert's perspective, there is a lot to explain, so

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:17.950
use cases that seem obvious can be

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:19.810
omitted.

00:11:20.770 --> 00:11:23.460
So, what about from a developer's perspective?

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:28.290


00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:30.700
Wouldn't it be more helpful for document management to omit simple news cases like searching for theaters?

00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:32.870
If it's a backend design, it seems okay to omit

00:11:32.870 --> 00:11:35.920
simple use cases like searching for a theater. It's

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:37.760


00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:41.570
not too late to respond during the implementation phase.

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:44.310
However, for an actual project,

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:48.450
these use cases are a good starting point for the screen planner. The

00:11:49.250 --> 00:11:52.300
planner can look at the use case for searching for a theater and think about

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:55.300
how to enable searching for a theater. When you

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:56.600


00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:00.880
merge the diagrams, it looks complicated. In an

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:05.150
actual project, it would be much more complicated. In

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:08.240
this case, there is no need to forcefully merge them. I

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:13.910
merged them here just to reveal that there are missing cases. I

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:18.490
filled in all the missing cases.

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.880
There is a use case where the connection to the actor is omitted.

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:25.720
This means that it applies to both the customer and the manager.

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:29.480
And I wrote it in English for the convenience of implementation. It's

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:34.160
okay because it's a small project now, but

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:36.160
if the project is large and complex, the

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:38.980
domain expert may be at a loss as to where to start explaining.

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:40.380


00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:43.300
What does a manager do?  It's

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:44.700
a lot of work, as expected from an

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:46.620
administrator. In

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:49.750
this case, it's a

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:52.340
good idea to consider whether the actors can be subdivided. Although it

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:54.790
was initially called an administrator, it may be possible to

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:57.400
subdivide by role.

00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:00.820
And there may be an external service that uses the Movie Booking System. If the

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:02.870


00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:05.340
actors are subdivided like this, the

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:09.420
domain expert will be able to provide information in a more systematic way.

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:14.000
Of course, this is just one example of analyzing a complex domain. There may be many

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:17.320
other good methods. It is

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:21.740
important to respond flexibly according to the situation.

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:25.060
Conclusion: In

00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:29.170
this session, we used a use case diagram to

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:30.840
set the project's goals and

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:33.700
proceeded with the initial stages of analysis.

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:38.010
Personally, I think analysis is the most difficult part of the entire development process. This is

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:40.060


00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:44.250
because the most abstract and vague concepts need to be organized.

00:13:45.020 --> 00:13:47.650
Next time, we will analyze the main use cases in

00:13:47.650 --> 00:13:49.470
more detail. Thank you for your hard work

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:52.100
so far.

